export const debate = {"id":"huemer-lutz-morality-real-2026","number":"56","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Mike Huemer vs Matt Lutz: Is Morality Real?","label":"Moral realism and error theory","date":"2026-08-23","duration":"1 hr 42 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-w2auo-Y5Wg","motion":"Are there objective moral facts, and can moral knowledge be justified by appearances rather than only by best-explanation evidence?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Huemer defends objective morality through evaluative judgment and defeasible appearances; Lutz counters with desire-based reasons, projectivism, and explanationist standards for knowledge.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"the plausibility of those judgments is higher than the initial plausibility","context":"Huemer compares elementary judgments about suffering and gratuitous torture with the premises supporting moral anti-realism."},"con":{"text":"the only reasons that exist are desired dependent reasons","context":"Lutz states the universal desire-dependence premise within his conditional argument from Humean reasons to moral error theory."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Moral realism","speaker":"Michael Huemer","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Moral error theory","speaker":"Matt Lutz","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":88,"con":81},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-objective-value-and-error-theory","title":"Objective Value and the Error-Theoretic Burden","timebox":"0:58–29:49","score":{"pro":89,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-objective-basic-moral-plausibility","time":"11:53","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Elementary judgments that suffering is bad and gratuitous torture is wrong are initially more plausible than the desire-dependent premises used to support moral error theory.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly compares the credibility of error theory's decisive premises with concrete elementary moral judgments, maintaining careful comparative language rather than claiming an immediate deductive refutation. Principal limitation: Initial plausibility supplies defeasible evidence but cannot independently settle whether an error theory ultimately explains why such judgments seem compelling despite their alleged systematic falsity. Live burden: The realist must connect comparative intuitive credibility to objective truth while answering genealogical, projectivist, and explanatory accounts capable of preserving phenomenological force without moral facts. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding responsiveness, precise qualification, high representational charity, and direct motion contact, with a bounded deduction because the argument establishes epistemic priority more securely than the complete metaphysical conclusion.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-objective-values-denied","time":"0:58","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Moral valuing arises through human attitudes and social practices of praise and blame, while the universe supplies no independent standards ratifying those values.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The opening clearly identifies attitude-independence as the decisive metaphysical issue and connects ordinary moral valuing with recognizable interpersonal practices of praise, blame, endorsement, and criticism. Principal limitation: The descriptive observation that valuing is socially embedded does not independently establish the categorical conclusion that objective evaluative standards are nonexistent, leaving the evidential warrant materially underdeveloped. Live burden: The position must demonstrate why social causation and objective correctness are incompatible, rather than merely showing that human attitudes participate extensively in moral cognition and communication. Locked score: The strong but limited band reflects excellent motion-level relevance and a recoverable alternative account, substantially moderated by overstated certainty, compressed inference, and insufficient positive support for ontological denial.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-humean-error-theory-inference","time":"25:08","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If every reason depends upon desires and moral facts entail desire-independent reasons, moral facts do not exist; the controversial premise requires comparative worldview assessment.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply presents the Humean argument in an explicit valid form, defines desires broadly enough to include values and goals, and acknowledges that worldview comparison remains necessary. Principal limitation: Restating the conditional structure does not answer the strongest counterexamples alleging that evaluative assessment determines which desires possess authority, so the universal premise remains insufficiently supported. Live burden: The error theorist must provide noncircular grounds for desire dependence and explain why elementary moral judgments should be overturned after comparative assessment rather than treated as direct counterevidence. Locked score: The strong competent band rewards excellent motion relevance, coherent inferential organization, and reasonable representation of the plausibility dispute, while reflecting incomplete responsiveness and evidential weakness concerning the argument's indispensable universal premise.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-desires-reasons-and-rational-authority","title":"Desires, Reasons, and Rational Authority","timebox":"4:12–41:28","score":{"pro":91,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evaluative-assessment-of-desires","time":"16:51","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Desire constraints cannot alone distinguish rational from irrational choices among present, future, imagined, or higher-order desires; that distinction requires an evaluative judgment.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response confronts desire reduction through varied counterexamples and identifies the precise explanatory gap between possessing differently situated preferences and rationally determining which preference deserves practical authority. Principal limitation: Demonstrating that ordinary deliberation employs evaluative judgments does not by itself establish that those judgments correspond to attitude-independent facts rather than sophisticated structures within an agent's standpoint. Live burden: The realist must show that ranking rules cannot be constituted by informed, coherent, stable, future-directed, or reflectively endorsed preferences without presupposing the disputed objective evaluation. Locked score: The very strong band reflects unusually direct burden contact, strong coherence, matched calibration, and charitable testing of sophisticated alternatives, with only a limited deduction because the metaphysical transition beyond desire insufficiency remains incomplete.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-desire-based-reasons","time":"4:12","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Moral obligations require desire-independent categorical reasons, yet intelligible reasons depend on an agent's broadly construed desires, values, or goals, so categorical obligations do not exist.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument supplies a transparent conditional pathway from desire-dependent reasons to moral error theory, defining categorical normativity sufficiently clearly for the subsequent disagreement to remain focused. Principal limitation: The universal premise that every intelligible reason derives from desires, values, goals, or deliberative standpoints is asserted more confidently than the accompanying considerations can substantiate. Live burden: The account must explain why reflective evaluation, rational criticism of desires, and conflicts among present, future, higher-order, and counterfactual preferences introduce no genuinely independent normative standard. Locked score: The strong competent band recognizes coherent structure, indispensable relevance, and conceptual precision, while preserving substantial deductions for incomplete warrant and the absence of an independently defended explanation of universal desire dependence.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-self-conception-authority-objection","time":"37:51","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Neither centrality nor higher-order status automatically grants rational authority, because spontaneous desires to revise other desires can be equally arbitrary and unsupported.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The objection isolates the missing authority principle and tests it with a precisely targeted case in which a baseless second-order urge possesses formal hierarchy without greater rational credibility. Principal limitation: The counterexample defeats authority by order alone more decisively than sophisticated accounts grounded in stable identification, informed endorsement, diachronic coherence, or constitutive practical identity. Live burden: The realist must show that every improved self-conception criterion still presupposes objective evaluation, rather than merely demonstrating that one simplistic hierarchy cannot distinguish rational commitment from arbitrary impulse. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional diagnostic precision, responsiveness, logical coherence, and charitable engagement, with a small reservation because the broader conclusion requires systematic treatment of stronger hierarchical and constitutivist alternatives.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-self-conception-normative-weight","time":"29:47","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Normative weight need not track motivational strength; it can instead reflect how centrally a desire or commitment figures within the agent's conception of self.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly blocks the inference from overpowering motivation to superior normative reason by rejecting proportionalism and supplying self-conception as a more discriminating hierarchical alternative. Principal limitation: Centrality, stability, and identification are psychologically intelligible properties, but the move does not adequately explain how any of them generates rational authority rather than motivational organization. Live burden: The theory must distinguish authoritative self-defining commitments from entrenched prejudice, arbitrary higher-order preference, adaptive distortion, or coherent wickedness without importing an external evaluative criterion. Locked score: The strong competent band reflects high responsiveness, useful conceptual refinement, and close contact with the indispensable desire-based bridge, moderated by partly unstable terminology and an underdeveloped inference from personal centrality to normative weight.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-appearances-explanation-and-justification","title":"Appearances, Explanation, and Epistemic Foundations","timebox":"8:27–81:58","score":{"pro":90,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-appearance-prima-facie-justification","time":"15:26","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Appearances provide defeasible prima facie justification when undefeated, because denying every appearance-based source would leave that denial unsupported by its own epistemic basis.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The self-defeat diagnosis targets explanationist exclusivity at its foundation and carefully restricts appearance-based support to prima facie justification operating only when relevant defeaters are absent. Principal limitation: The argument compresses the controversial premise that every opposing epistemological belief ultimately rests upon appearances in the contentful justificatory sense required by the conclusion. Live burden: The defender must distinguish intellectual appearances from confidence, sensation, coherence, and causal-model acceptance while explaining how appearance-based entitlement avoids indiscriminate credulity or circular self-certification. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes exceptional responsiveness, conceptual stability, charitable engagement, and an important foundational challenge, while retaining a modest deduction for the underdeveloped transition from unavoidable seeming to proposition-specific epistemic justification.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-explanationist-moral-skepticism","time":"8:27","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Beliefs receive evidential support when their truth best explains experience; objective moral truths perform no comparable explanatory work and therefore cannot ground moral knowledge.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move articulates a unified explanationist criterion and applies it directly to moral knowledge, giving the skeptical route a concrete epistemological mechanism instead of relying upon metaphysical disbelief. Principal limitation: Generalizing from perceptual examples to the necessity of best-explanation support throughout every epistemic domain considerably exceeds what the coffee-cup illustration and moral application themselves establish. Live burden: The account must justify explanatory indispensability as a universal condition while addressing mathematical, logical, introspective, and foundational knowledge that may not depend upon causal explanation. Locked score: The strong competent band reflects exceptional route relevance and clearly recoverable reasoning, tempered by materially overstated generality, limited independent warrant, and insufficient engagement with defeasible appearance-based justification as a potentially complementary epistemic source.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-appearances-prior-to-explanation","time":"79:14","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Assessing causal or best explanations presupposes initial acceptance that things generally are as they appear, because background beliefs otherwise generate an unresolved justificatory regress.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply exposes a foundational regress in appealing to background causal beliefs and explains why judging one explanation superior appears to presuppose epistemic access to experiential data. Principal limitation: The argument does not fully exclude coherentist models in which appearances, causal beliefs, and explanatory relations receive reciprocal support without a uniquely privileged foundational source. Live burden: The defender must specify what initial acceptance contributes, how strongly it justifies proposition-specific belief, and how defeaters constrain it without relying upon the explanatory structures declared epistemically posterior. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional diagnostic responsiveness, matched calibration, conceptual stability, and central route importance, retaining only a modest reservation concerning coherentist alternatives and the exact strength of initial appearance-based entitlement.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-causal-perceptual-justification","time":"74:53","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Perceptual experience supports belief through background causal understanding connecting experiences with worldly sources, not through propositional content that independently provides initial justification.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response supplies a concrete causal alternative to content-based justification and applies it clearly to ordinary perception, directly addressing the appearance principle's proposed epistemic mechanism. Principal limitation: Showing that causal background beliefs contribute substantially to perceptual justification does not establish their exclusivity or exclude defeasible initial support from experiential content. Live burden: The explanationist must account for the justification of background causal beliefs themselves and extend the model beyond perception without initiating regress, circularity, or dependence upon accepted appearances. Locked score: The very strong band reflects unusually direct contact, conceptual clarity, strong responsiveness, and a well-developed competing mechanism, with deductions for slightly overstated exclusivity and insufficient demonstration that causal mediation displaces rather than supplements appearance-based entitlement.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-moral-belief-emotion-and-projection","title":"Moral Belief, Emotion, and Projection","timebox":"56:03–67:00","score":{"pro":83,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-moral-belief-shapes-motivation","time":"56:03","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Abandoning objective moral belief could substantially change desires, emotions, charitable choices, and interpretations of conduct, even if personality continued restraining aggression or theft.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response supplies a concrete counterfactual mechanism through which objective moral belief can affect charity, interpretation, emotion, and conduct rather than functioning as motivationally idle projection. Principal limitation: Its evidential foundation is predominantly introspective and predictive, making the extension from one speaker's anticipated reactions to broader psychological tendencies considerably less secure. Live burden: The realist must separate the causal contribution of objective belief from associated identity, habits, communities, and enduring preferences, ideally through evidence extending beyond personal phenomenological expectation. Locked score: The strong competent band recognizes clear responsiveness, conceptual relevance, explicit qualification, and a plausible challenge to one-way projectivism, while reflecting limited generalizability, comparatively modest bridge importance, and insufficient independent evidence for population-level motivational effects.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-moral-projection","time":"58:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Antecedent values are projected onto the world as apparent external endorsement, so rejecting objective morality ordinarily need not eliminate the underlying attitudes or conduct.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move answers the motivational counterfactual with an intelligible causal alternative in which antecedent values generate moral belief through projection and may persist after metaphysical rejection. Principal limitation: Establishing that prior attitudes can cause moral belief does not demonstrate that the resulting belief lacks reciprocal influence upon emotion, interpretation, preference formation, or subsequent conduct. Live burden: The projectivist must independently substantiate both the proposed causal ordering and the predicted behavioral stability while accommodating cases where reflective moral judgment apparently redirects stronger emotional attraction. Locked score: The strong competent band rewards direct responsiveness, stable formulation, and a coherent subsidiary mechanism, tempered by limited empirical warrant and a slightly overstated inference from possible antecedent causation to ordinary motivational persistence.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-judgment-directs-emotion-conduct","time":"62:39","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Intellectual judgments can override emotionally attractive options, as cost-effectiveness can direct charity, while judgments about injustice can subsequently shape motivating emotions and intervention.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply gives two differentiated counterexamples, one involving judgment selecting conduct against emotional attraction and another involving evaluative belief shaping the emotions that later motivate intervention. Principal limitation: Personal examples establish psychological possibility more securely than a general causal model, and an opponent can reinterpret low-affect intellectual commitments as stable antecedent passions. Live burden: The realist must identify evidence distinguishing genuinely judgment-led motivation from less phenomenally salient preferences while explaining when cognition changes emotion rather than merely expressing established concerns. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent responsiveness, coherent modal calibration, and precise pressure upon one-way projection, with a measured deduction because the examples challenge exclusivity without fully establishing objective moral facts or widespread causal direction.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-emotion-precedes-judgment","time":"64:55","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Emotions generally precede moral judgments, and apparently unemotional deliberation may still express stable cold passions tied to the agent's enduring self-conception.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly engages the allegedly intellectual counterexamples by offering an emotion-first interpretation and distinguishing conspicuous hot emotions from stable, comparatively unobtrusive cold passions. Principal limitation: Expanding passion to cover every enduring motivating commitment threatens to redescribe the disputed phenomenon after the fact rather than independently establishing antecedent causal priority. Live burden: The account must provide discriminating empirical criteria for identifying cold passions before judgment and explain cases where changed evaluative belief predictably reorganizes emotional response or conduct. Locked score: The strong competent band reflects substantial responsiveness, relevant conceptual resources, and appropriate probabilistic framing, while registering weaker coherence and warrant arising from partly unstable terminology and insufficient adjudication of the competing causal sequence.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-apriori-knowledge-concepts-and-analyticity","title":"A Priori Knowledge, Concepts, and Analyticity","timebox":"90:04–101:57","score":{"pro":84,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-synthetic-apriori-color-incompatibility","time":"92:20","role":"Major direct reply","words":"The necessary incompatibility of being wholly red and wholly blue appears knowable a priori yet synthetic, because synonym substitution does not reduce it to logic.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply presents a familiar, sharply specified candidate for necessary a priori knowledge and develops a concrete substitution test challenging reduction to linguistic or conceptual analyticity. Principal limitation: Failure under an austere synonym-based criterion does not establish that every defensible broader account of analyticity must fail or that the necessity is grounded independently of concepts. Live burden: The rationalist must provide a principled, noncircular criterion distinguishing substantive insight into phenomenal natures from competent mastery of constraints governing color-concept application. Locked score: The very strong band reflects coherent argumentation, explicit qualification, strong responsiveness, and an effective counterexample, with bounded deductions because the proposed analyticity test is not demonstrably exhaustive and the issue supports only a subsidiary route.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-mathematical-concept-application","time":"90:04","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Mathematical reflection provides experiential evidence through felt confidence and conceptual application, rather than through intellectual seemings whose propositional content directly supplies justification.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move accepts mathematical reflection as evidentially significant while offering a naturalistic phenomenological reinterpretation, thereby avoiding the implausible claim that mathematical reasoning provides no evidence whatsoever. Principal limitation: Reclassifying an intellectual seeming as sensation or confidence does not explain why that experience supports one specific mathematical proposition rather than merely reporting psychological conviction. Live burden: The empiricist account must connect conceptual competence, experienced relations, and truth-conducive reliability without quietly restoring proposition-directed appearance under alternative terminology. Locked score: The strong competent band reflects cautious calibration, relevant engagement, and a recoverable alternative, while recognizing compressed mechanism, partly unstable distinctions, limited bridge importance, and insufficient warrant for the transition from felt correctness to justified mathematical belief.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-natures-ground-moral-apriori-knowledge","time":"97:03","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"A priori knowledge concerns the natures of things, not merely words or concepts; understanding suffering and badness can therefore justify judging suffering bad.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The closing argument unifies the color discussion with moral epistemology by distinguishing knowledge of represented natures from knowledge merely concerning words, concepts, or permissible applications. Principal limitation: The transition from necessary color incompatibility to suffering's badness requires an additional substantive bridge, while the metaphysical and epistemological notion of a nature remains incompletely specified. Live burden: The realist must explain how reflection reliably discriminates object-grounded evaluative necessity from entrenched conceptual structure, affective response, or culturally stabilized association. Locked score: The strong competent band recognizes direct contact with both conceptualist alternatives, high responsiveness, and indispensable moral application, moderated by slightly overstated extension, partially unstable terminology, and insufficient independent defense of the crucial nature-based bridge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-color-incompatibility-analytic","time":"93:30","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Complete red-blue incompatibility is analytic in a broad sense because recognizing inconsistent joint application manifests competent understanding of the relevant phenomenal color concepts.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response directly answers the flagship counterexample, explains why austere synonym-based analysis may be unnecessarily restrictive, and proposes competent understanding as a broader criterion of analyticity. Principal limitation: Treating recognition of necessary incompatibility as evidence of conceptual understanding does not determine whether the necessity is concept-grounded or instead reflects mind-independent phenomenal natures. Live burden: The conceptualist must formulate a noncircular criterion separating analytic constraints from substantive necessities that competent thinkers recognize through understanding but do not create through conceptual rules. Locked score: The strong competent band rewards excellent responsiveness, coherent reframing, and substantial charity, while preserving deductions for partly unstable terminology, slightly overstated classification, and the absence of a sufficiently discriminating account of broadened analyticity.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":88,"strengths":["Directly challenged error theory by comparing its premises with concrete judgments about suffering and gratuitous torture.","Exposed the unresolved authority problem for desire hierarchies through the arbitrary higher-order desire counterexample.","Pressed a foundational regress against exclusive explanationism and defended appearances as defeasible rather than indefeasible.","Used differentiated motivation cases to show that judgment can select conduct and shape subsequent emotion.","Connected the a priori dispute back to moral knowledge through a proposed distinction between concepts and represented natures."],"blunders":[{"text":"The motivation argument relied heavily on personal prediction and did not independently establish broader causal tendencies.","links":[]},{"text":"The final appeal to natures left the bridge from color incompatibility to the badness of suffering insufficiently developed.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":81,"strengths":["Presented a clear conditional route from universal desire dependence to denial of objective moral facts.","Improved the desire account by rejecting motivational proportionalism and proposing self-conception as a ranking mechanism.","Offered a concrete causal explanation of perceptual justification using background beliefs about worldly sources.","Answered motivational realism with projectivist and cold-passion models rather than simple denial.","Developed a broad analyticity response directly addressing the red-blue incompatibility counterexample."],"blunders":[{"text":"The categorical denial of objective value outran the descriptive evidence about socially embedded valuing.","links":[]},{"text":"The universal desire-dependence premise remained unsupported after the evaluative and arbitrary-hierarchy objections.","links":[]},{"text":"Explanationism was generalized beyond perception without resolving how its background beliefs first acquire justification.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest realist case treats objective moral reasons as the best account of rational criticism across changing desires, with defeasible appearances providing initial but revisable access to elementary evaluative truths.","premises":["Agents can rationally criticize present, future, higher-order, and identity-defining desires, so the mere location or strength of a desire cannot exhaust normative authority.","The most economical common explanation of such criticism is that some considerations count in favor of action independently of whichever desire presently dominates.","Appearances can supply initial justification without guaranteeing truth, provided their force is reduced by disagreement, causal debunking, incoherence, and contrary evidence.","Elementary judgments about gratuitous suffering remain comparatively stable under informed reflection and therefore deserve evidential weight unless a stronger defeating explanation is supplied.","Judgment-led changes in motivation are compatible with emotions and desires also influencing judgment, so realism needs reciprocal causation rather than cognition-only motivation."],"conclusion":"Objective moral reasons are a credible explanation of rational criticism, and carefully defeasible appearances can justify limited elementary moral beliefs, although neither claim excludes revision or further naturalistic explanation."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Cross-standpoint correction","text":"A desire theory can explain why an agent endorses a commitment, but rational criticism often persists when every currently endorsed preference favors the mistake. Cases involving manipulated identities, adaptive preferences, or coherent cruelty suggest that stability and self-centrality are not sufficient for authority. Objective reasons offer a unified explanation: standpoint features affect access and motivation, while the consideration's normative force need not be created by endorsement. This does not prove realism, but it identifies explanatory work that a purely internal account must reproduce without covert evaluation."},{"title":"Defeater-sensitive convergence","text":"Appearance-based justification becomes more discriminating when paired with explicit defeaters. An elementary moral appearance gains credibility when it survives factual correction, imaginative variation, awareness of framing, and scrutiny from differently situated agents; it loses credibility when those checks reveal distortion. This model neither treats seeming as certainty nor requires best-explanation reasoning to create all initial justification. It instead assigns explanatory reasoning a crucial revising role, thereby preserving the foundational insight while answering the concern that appearances indiscriminately validate whatever anyone happens to feel."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest error-theoretic case explains normative practice through structured agent-relative reasons and treats moral appearances as psychologically informative but insufficient evidence for attitude-independent moral facts.","premises":["Practical reasons can be indexed to an agent's informed and coherent concerns without making momentary motivational strength the measure of normative weight.","Self-conception has authority only when it survives information, consistency, diachronic stability, and scrutiny of manipulation, rather than merely occupying a higher psychological level.","Moral experience can result from projection, social learning, emotional salience, and concept acquisition even if moral belief later reciprocally influences motivation.","Evidence for a distinctive domain of facts should increase when those facts contribute explanatory or predictive success beyond the psychological and social mechanisms already available.","Intellectual confidence and conceptual competence can explain some nonperceptual justification without establishing that every forceful seeming directly represents an independent fact."],"conclusion":"Moral error theory remains credible if structured standpoint-relative reasons explain practical criticism and naturalistic mechanisms explain moral appearances without additional predictive contribution from objective moral facts."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Counterfactual explanatory contribution","text":"Instead of requiring every justified belief to arise from best explanation, the skeptic can ask a narrower question: what observable or inferential difference would objective moral facts make after psychological dispositions, social learning, factual beliefs, and deliberative consistency are held fixed? If rival realist theories predict conflicting obligations while sharing the same ordinary evidence, their additional facts may have limited discriminating power. This argument does not show that explanatory idleness entails falsity; it supplies a defeater against claiming knowledge until some distinctive contribution is identified."},{"title":"Procedural authority without categorical reasons","text":"The self-conception proposal can be strengthened procedurally. A commitment gains practical authority when it is informed, coherent with enduring concerns, resistant to manipulation, and endorsed across temporally separated deliberative perspectives. These conditions remain grounded in the agent while excluding spontaneous higher-order urges and many adaptive distortions. The account still permits external criticism by showing that a person has false factual beliefs, procedural inconsistency, or commitments that frustrate other stable concerns. Its remaining burden is to explain apparently rational criticism of a fully informed and coherent but morally abhorrent standpoint."}]}},"year":2026,"topicCategory":"moral-realism-objectivity"};
